nodecode-household-tiers is a rewrite of chevlume's chevlume/household-tiers:

  https://github.com/chevlume/household-tiers
  commit 1896b63f63c8f1615d974e30c3c731e895fb8d70
  MIT License, Copyright (c) 2026 Chevlume (LICENSE)

Every line of Common Lisp here is new. What was rewritten: the plugin's
policy document, read in the same shape (sessions, default, tiers, hours,
audit), its tier check before a tool call, its quiet hours with a window that
may cross midnight, its audit ledger with retention and /family forget, and
its /family digest, here /household-tiers with the same three verbs and the
same owner-only rule. The check that ran in the gateway's pre-tool-call hook
runs here on Nodecode's :tool point, so the refusal is the failed call the
model reads, and the ledger and the policy live under the Nodecode home
instead of the Hermes home.

What was left out, and why:

- The owner's memory withholding. The plugin rewrote every non-owner model
  request -- stripping the boxed MEMORY.md / USER.md blocks, the external
  provider block joined after them and <memory-context> recalls, and refusing
  a turn whose boxed header survived. Nodecode's cells have a tool point and a
  frame point and no request-rewrite seam, so the rewrite keeps the tiers, the
  hours and the audit, and the section's tiers should simply leave `memory`
  out of a non-owner list. The README says so.
- The file_roots path gate for read_file and search_files, and the
  local-network hostname check for the web tools. Both exist because a
  non-owner tier could be granted those tools; Nodecode's tools are `eval`
  and whatever a cell registers, and `eval` has full host authority whichever
  tier calls it, so neither gate would bound anything a tier could not step
  around.
- The curfew as a whole-turn answer. The plugin stopped a quiet-hours turn at
  the gateway and answered it itself; here the turn runs and its tool calls
  are what wait, which is the seam a cell has.
- The parent alert. The plugin asked the gateway adapter to send a message to
  a chat it named; a Nodecode cell would need a channel cell's outbound seam,
  and the entry never depends on another entry.
- The /familia alias and the Spanish default messages: the command name is
  the cell's own, so there is one spelling.
